<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="
           http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
           http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-latest.xsd">

    <!--
      Premium-relative target / stop-loss bracket.

      Why a percentage and not more absolute points
      =============================================
      trade_config.target / stop_loss are absolute premium points, compared by
      PositionService.thresholdBreach against per-share option P&L. That works
      for a config that trades one premium level. It does not work for a config
      that trades a *band*: 024/025 set the standing band at 80-250, a 3x spread,
      and no single points value serves both ends. A 30-point target is a 12%
      move at 250 and a 38% move at 80; a 120-point target - what the EOD
      detector was writing off ATR - is unreachable at any entry below 120 and
      needs the premium to go to zero at 250.

      Measured on the imported Jan-2024 NIFTY option series (every 5-min candle
      09:20-14:30 as a hypothetical short entry, premium inside the band, ATM +-3
      strikes, front expiry, first touch on 5-min high/low):

        bracket                    TARGET    STOP    ran to force close
        120/120 pts (1x ATR)          3.6%   10.1%              86.3%
        30/30 pts                    45.4%   44.5%              10.1%
        20% / 30% of entry premium   53.9%   33.7%              12.4%

      The points bracket does not fail gracefully - at 1x ATR it stops being an
      exit rule at all and 86% of trades run to force close. Scaling the bracket
      to the premium the trade actually opened at is what keeps it reachable
      across the whole band.

      Semantics
      =========
      NULL = "use the absolute target / stop_loss column", which is what every
      existing MANUAL config keeps doing - this changeset is behaviour-neutral
      for them. When set, OrderService snapshots entryPrice * pct into
      trade_order.target_at_entry / stop_loss_at_entry at open, so the bracket is
      frozen per trade exactly like the absolute one already is, and
      PositionService needs no change.

      DECIMAL(6,4): a fraction, not a display percentage. 0.2000 = 20%.

      The sma_downtrend_rule copy gets defaults so the generated configs pick the
      premium-relative bracket up immediately; that table is NOT NULL throughout
      (see 026) for the same reason - a null there would silently fall back to
      the ATR points bracket this changeset exists to replace.

      trade_config stays nullable with no default: a hand-made config opts in.
    -->
    <changeSet id="027_add_pct_bracket_to_trade_config" author="moneymaker">
        <addColumn tableName="trade_config">
            <column name="target_pct" type="DECIMAL(6,4)"/>
            <column name="sl_pct" type="DECIMAL(6,4)"/>
        </addColumn>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="027_add_pct_bracket_to_sma_downtrend_rule" author="moneymaker">
        <addColumn tableName="sma_downtrend_rule">
            <column name="target_pct" type="DECIMAL(6,4)" defaultValueNumeric="0.20">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <column name="sl_pct" type="DECIMAL(6,4)" defaultValueNumeric="0.30">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
        </addColumn>
    </changeSet>

    <!--
      Retune the multipliers that size the absolute fallback bracket.

      1.0/1.0 came from 018's column defaults and was never chosen: it made
      target and stop-loss identical, and on the old true-range basis it produced
      brackets of 77-158 premium points on legs entered between 80 and 250.

      The basis has since changed (EodDowntrendDetectionService now measures the
      traded leg's mean intraday range, not the detected leg's ATR), which alone
      brings the same six Jan-2024 configs from 77-158 down to 84-155 of basis.
      Multiplying by 0.30 / 0.45 lands them at 25-47 target against 38-70 stop —
      inside what an ATM leg actually travels in a session, and asymmetric in the
      direction the payoff is: a short option's gain is capped at the premium
      while its loss is not.

      Scoped to rows still holding the 1.0 default so a deliberately chosen
      multiplier is not overwritten.
    -->
    <changeSet id="027_retune_downtrend_multipliers" author="moneymaker">
        <update tableName="sma_downtrend_rule">
            <column name="target_multiplier" valueNumeric="0.30"/>
            <where>target_multiplier = 1.0</where>
        </update>
        <update tableName="sma_downtrend_rule">
            <column name="sl_multiplier" valueNumeric="0.45"/>
            <where>sl_multiplier = 1.0</where>
        </update>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
